 {"id": "CF-226", "title": "deepseek-harness (dsh) is real and absent from both repos \u2014 an installer at a pinned RC, and the spec sentence that denied it exists is corrected", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 1, "reasoning": "CORRECTION 2026-11-05: this row originally read 'there is no deepseek harness'. That was true of our two trees and false of the world. DeepSeek Harness is deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness, CLI dsh, npm @deepseek-ai/dsh 0.2.1-rc.5 (bin dsh -> lib/bin.js), built on the Cordis plugin architecture and documented as 'everything is a plugin'; it runs via npx @deepseek-ai/dsh web or from source with pnpm. Neither crier nor code-factory mentions it: crier's adapter set is a closed list of four constants (internal/harness/config.go:35) and the only 'deepseek' in the tree is a guard provider preset. specs/HARNESS-MATRIX.md now lists it with the correction recorded rather than silently edited.", "acceptance_criteria": "1. scripts/harness/install-dsh.sh --check/--apply installs the PINNED @deepseek-ai/dsh under $HOME, reads 'dsh --version' back, and is idempotent; the pin is an RC, so the row states how the version is re-measured.\n2. The installer accepts the CF_* contract (specs/HARNESS-MATRIX.md section 5) and refuses a credential passed as a value.\n3. One real turn produces a known artifact and reports model_served; a plugin-layer failure is reported per layer (package -> resolved -> enabled -> service -> tool -> permission) rather than as 'dsh is broken'.\n4. specs/HARNESS-MATRIX.md and specs/HARNESS.md both list dsh with its measured state, and no document claims a deepseek harness does not exist.\n5. A crier adapter (or an explicit written decision not to add one) is recorded; without it the harness can only be driven by the node's own worker.", "probe": null, "created_at": "2026-11-05T03:14:21Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T03:14:21Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "f210f6cdebb1c493efccb6cc6463eaff87e1cc061f177b89be19e1569cacf39c"},